// Call the printEndpoints() method to print out the list of endpoints
hatchedKoa.printEndpoints();
```

By running this code in your application, you will see a list of all the endpoints that have been generated by Hatchify based on your schemas. This can help you understand the API routes available in your application and how they are structured.", "How can I setup custom validation for an endpoint?": "To set up custom validation for an endpoint in your Hatchify application, you can leverage the validation hooks provided by Hatchify. These hooks allow you to define custom validation logic for your resources.

Here's a general outline of how you can set up custom validation for an endpoint:

1. Define your schema with the necessary attributes and validation functions. For example, you can define a schema for an `Employee` resource with custom validation logic:

```typescript
export const Employee = {
 name: "Employee",
 attributes: {
 firstName: string(),
 lastName: string(),
 startDate: datetime(),
 endDate: datetime(),
 },
 validation: {
 startDateBeforeEndDate() {
 const { startDate, endDate } = this as unknown as { startDate: Date; endDate: Date };
 if (startDate && endDate && startDate > endDate) {
 throw [
 new UnexpectedValueError({
 detail: "Start date cannot be after end date.",
 pointer: "data/attributes/startDate",
 }),
 ];
 }
 },
 },
} satisfies PartialSchema;
```

2. Implement the custom validation logic within the validation function defined in your schema. In this example, the `startDateBeforeEndDate` function checks if the `startDate` is before the `endDate`.

3. Use the custom validation function in your endpoint logic. You can call this validation function before creating or updating a resource to ensure data integrity.

By following these steps, you can easily set up custom validation for your endpoints in Hatchify, ensuring that your data meets the required criteria before being processed.", "How can I define an attribute as an optional integer?": "To define an attribute as an optional integer, you can use the `integer` function from the Hatchify open-source project in TypeScript. Here is an example of how you can define an optional integer attribute:

